  SO, EXACTLY: (A2-PATH) is refuted AS STATED (it carries no hypotheses at all,
  and r30 recorded it with none), AND it is refuted under rad(G) >= 5 as well.
  The ONE Problem A hypothesis no witness here carries is l > 4.
  What that settles is the SHAPE of any possible proof:
      a proof of (A2-PATH) MUST consume l > 4, because everything else in
      Problem A's hypothesis list is already satisfied by a counterexample.
      And the failure is not marginal: path(G) does not grow AT ALL while h = 1.
      Note the mismatch this exposes -- l > 4 is a DENSITY hypothesis and the
      failure is POSITIONAL (the hair hangs where no longest induced path ends).

=== PART 5 -- THE ANCHORED REPLACEMENT, AND IT IS WEAKER THAN (A2-PATH) ===
  Route A2 does not need (A2-PATH).  It needs  path(G) >= rad(G) + 4,  and it
  already has  rad(G) <= rad(G') + h  (r30).  So it is ENOUGH to prove

      (A2-ANCHOR)   path(G)  >=  rad(G') + 4 + h.

  And for a hair of length h reaching G' at w, path(G) >= h + endpath(G', w).
  So (A2-ANCHOR) follows from an ANCHORED F11:

      (F11-AT-w)   endpath(G', w)  >=  rad(G') + 4     for the attachment w
                   of a DEEPEST hair.

  F11 gives that number for SOME endpoint of G'.  (F11-AT-w) asks for it at a
  PRESCRIBED endpoint.  That is the real content of A2, it is strictly weaker
  than (A2-PATH), and it is where rad >= 5 / l > 4 would have to enter.

      on the witness: rad(G') = 2, endpath(G',w) = 4, rad(G')+4 = 6
  [PASS] the witness ALSO fails (F11-AT-w) -- consistent, and it shows the two forms fail together rather than the anchored one being vacuous  -- 4 < 6
      NOTE the witness fails F11's own hypotheses too (rad(G') = 2 < 5), so this
      is a consistency observation, NOT evidence against (F11-AT-w).  Nothing
      here proves or refutes the anchored form; it is proposed, not claimed.
